  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "An isosceles triangle has an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). Must it be an equilateral triangle?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Not necessarily. When the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle is a base angle, the triangle is not equilateral.",
    "B": "Not necessarily. When the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle is the vertex angle, the triangle is not equilateral.",
    "C": "Yes. Whether the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle is the vertex angle or a base angle, all three angles are \\(60^\\circ\\).",
    "D": "Definitely not, because only the two legs of an isosceles triangle are equal."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "When the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle is the vertex angle, each base angle is \\(60^\\circ\\). When the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle is a base angle, the other base angle is also \\(60^\\circ\\), and the vertex angle is \\(60^\\circ\\).",
